<section class="doc-card">
<p class="pro-group-label" id="pro-diagnostics">Diagnostics</p>

<h3>Negotiation Diagnostics <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>Find out which part of the connection is holding it back: your Mac’s port, the cable or the connected device.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>“Negotiated” means the speed or power the connected devices have agreed to use. WhatCable compares this with the reported capabilities of each part, and identifies a limit when the available evidence supports it.</p></details>

<figure>
  <img src="/screenshot-negotiation-diagnostics.webp" alt="Negotiation Diagnostics screen showing per-port power and data-speed breakdowns with the bottleneck highlighted" loading="lazy" width="480">
  <figcaption>Negotiation Diagnostics: what each part of the chain supports vs. what was actually negotiated.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Display Diagnostics <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>Find out why your screen is not as sharp or smooth as expected. Compare its picture settings with what the screen and its connection can support.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>Resolution describes picture detail; refresh rate is how often the picture updates. Display Diagnostics compares these with the monitor’s reported modes and the DisplayPort connection’s lanes and rate. It accounts for Display Stream Compression (DSC), which fits the picture into less bandwidth, and adapter information when available. A lower link rate can be appropriate for the selected picture setting.</p></details>

<figure>
  <img src="/screenshot-display-diagnostics.webp" alt="Display Diagnostics screen showing a monitor's top mode against what the DisplayPort link is carrying, with the adapter named and the shortfall highlighted" loading="lazy" width="480">
  <figcaption>Display Diagnostics: what your monitor can do vs what the link is carrying, with the limiting factor named.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>DP Alt Mode details <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>See how the picture travels from your Mac to the screen, along with the display information your Mac can read.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>DisplayPort Alt Mode carries DisplayPort video through USB-C. The details include lane count (the signal paths in use), link rate (their speed), and whether video travels directly or through a Thunderbolt tunnel. EDID is the monitor’s identity and capabilities data, which can include its model, manufacturer and year.</p></details>

<figure>
  <img src="/screenshot-dp-altmode.webp" alt="Pro diagnostics screen for a USB-C port driving a display, showing the device identity and a pin diagram with the DP four-lane routing" loading="lazy">
  <figcaption>Display details: device identity, pin diagram with lane routing, and port health on one screen.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="doc-card">
<p class="pro-group-label" id="pro-history">Cable history</p>

<h3>Cable history <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>
  Add a cable, give it a name, and WhatCable starts keeping a record of how it actually performs over time, not just a snapshot of the moment it's plugged in. It recognises the cable on later connections from its e-marker fingerprint and builds up a timeline: when you last used it, what it negotiated each time, and whether it has been misbehaving.
</p>
<p>
  You get a saved-cables list, an all-time summary per cable (best and typical data speed, charging power, fault counts), and a verdict right on the port card so you don't have to dig. A per-cable graph plots its data speed over time against its rated ceiling, with 7-day, 30-day, and all-time views. Cables you haven't added still get the live reading as before, they just aren't recorded. Cables with no e-marker chip at all can't be added: with nothing to recognise them by, there's no way to tell one from another on a later connection. Localised in 19 languages.
</p>

<figure>
  <img src="/screenshot-cable-history.webp" alt="Saved Cables screen showing a named Anker Thunderbolt cable with a Performing verdict, last seen one second ago, and History, Rename, and Delete actions" loading="lazy" width="480">
  <figcaption>The Saved Cables list: each cable you've added, with a verdict, when it was last seen, and quick actions.</figcaption>
</figure>

<figure>
  <img src="/screenshot-cable-history-graph.webp" alt="Cable history detail showing a named Anker Thunderbolt cable, its rated 40 Gbps reference line, and observed data-speed points over a 30-day window" loading="lazy" width="480">
  <figcaption>Per-cable history: data speed over time against the cable's rated ceiling. Switch between 7-day, 30-day, and all-time views.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="doc-card">
<p class="pro-group-label" id="pro-power">Power &amp; charging</p>

<h3>Live power metering <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>See how much power your Mac reports through each port, with readings updated every two seconds.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>Watts (W) measure power, amps (A) measure current and volts (V) measure voltage. Where live per-port readings are unavailable, WhatCable shows the agreed maximum instead. That limit is not the same as the power being used at that moment.</p></details>

<h3>PD contract inspector <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>See which charging options your charger offers and which one your Mac is using. This helps explain why a powerful charger may be charging at a lower rate.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>USB Power Delivery (PD) is how compatible devices agree charging power. The agreement is called a power contract. Power Data Objects (PDOs) describe the available charging profiles. The inspector shows voltage, current and available profiles, and flags capability mismatches.</p></details>

<h3>Power monitor window <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>Watch a chart of power and voltage over time while you try a charger or cable. Keep the window open beside your work.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>The chart shows the available per-port readings. Where the Mac does not provide live power draw, the displayed negotiated maximum is the agreed limit, not measured consumption.</p></details>

<figure>
  <img src="/screenshot-power-monitor.webp" alt="Power Monitor window showing USB-C port 1 drawing 5.9W on a live chart of watts, volts, and amps, with the System Power Input panel below" loading="lazy">
  <figcaption>Power Monitor in its own window, with live per-port watts, volts, and amps. On Macs without live per-port draw, the value shown is the negotiated maximum instead.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>PD event trace <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>Follow what happened when you connected a cable: the charging setup, any interruptions and the final agreement.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>The USB Power Delivery event trace shows the protocol messages and events reported for a port, including attachment, contract negotiation and hard resets that restart power negotiation.</p></details>

<h3>Cable resistance estimation <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>Estimate how much the cable resists the flow of electricity. This can help you investigate power loss while testing a cable.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>The estimate uses multiple power samples and is shown in milliohms (mΩ), a unit of electrical resistance. It is an estimate, not a direct measurement or proof that a cable is worn.</p></details>
</section>

<section class="doc-card">
<p class="pro-group-label" id="pro-health">Cable &amp; port health</p>

<h3>Port health counters <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>Look for repeated disconnects, restarts and electrical errors reported by a port. Changes over time can help you investigate an unreliable connection.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>Counters can include attach/detach events, hard resets, power-role swaps and internal communication or switching errors. A power-role swap changes which device supplies power. These are reported events, not a complete lifetime wear record or proof that the cable caused a fault.</p></details>

<figure>
  <img src="/screenshot-diagnostics.webp" alt="Pro diagnostics screen showing port health counters, liquid detection status, and the negotiated power contract for one port" loading="lazy">
  <figcaption>Pro diagnostics: port health counters, liquid detection state, and power contract on one screen.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Liquid detection <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>Check the liquid-detection state reported for a USB-C port when investigating an intermittent connection.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>WhatCable displays the available liquid-detection controller state, also called LDCM. This is a report from the Mac’s hardware, not a physical inspection of the port.</p></details>

<h3>CC advertisement level <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>See how much current the port tells a connected device it can supply.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>CC is the Configuration Channel, a USB-C control connection. Its advertised current level is a setup signal; it is separate from a later USB Power Delivery charging contract.</p></details>

<h3>Raw cable identity <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>Read the exact information reported by the cable’s identity chip, alongside WhatCable’s explanation.</p>
<details class="plain-detail"><summary>Technical detail</summary><p>The chip is called an e-marker. Its structured identity fields include Vendor Defined Objects (VDOs). These are useful when investigating a report or sharing diagnostic details with a developer.</p></details>
</section>

<section class="doc-card">
<p class="pro-group-label" id="pro-terminal">In the terminal and on the desktop</p>

<h3>CLI monitor mode <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>
  <code>whatcable --monitor</code> streams live power and cable state to your terminal. <code>whatcable --monitor-json</code> outputs the same data as JSON for scripting.
</p>

<h3>TUI dashboard (CLI) <span class="pro-badge">Pro</span></h3>
<p>
  A live full-screen terminal view of ports, power, and Thunderbolt, for the SSH and tmux crowd. Three screens: Overview, Negotiation, and Power. Tab cycles screens, arrow keys select ports, q quits. Run <code>whatcable --dashboard</code>.
</p>

